## Why This Question Is Asked
Tests candidates on practical lock-free allocator design with emphasis on per-core caching, ABA avoidance, and NUMA-aware padding. Also probes understanding of microbenchmarking for contention analysis.

## Key Concepts
- Lock-free memory allocation and per-core caches
- ABA avoidance with tagged pointers or version counters
- Cache-line alignment and padding to prevent false sharing
- NUMA-aware data placement and cross-core contention

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct Block {
    struct Block* next;
    uintptr_t tag;
    unsigned char data[128];
} Block;

static _Atomic(Block*) g_free_list = NULL;

static Block* fetch_chunk(void) {
    Block* chunk = aligned_alloc(64, 256 * sizeof(Block));
    for (int i = 0; i < 255; i++) {
        chunk[i].next = &chunk[i + 1];
        chunk[i].tag = 0;
    }
    chunk[255].next = NULL;
    chunk[255].tag = 0;
    return chunk;
}

static Block* alloc_block(void) {
    Block* b = local_cache;  // thread-local
    do {
        if (b) return b;
    } while ((b = atomic_load_explicit(&g_free_list, memory_order_acquire)) 
             && !atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&g_free_list, &b, b->next));
    return b ? b : fetch_chunk();
}
```

## Microbenchmark Plan
1. **Baseline**: Measure allocation latency with warm local cache
2. **Contention test**: Scale threads from 8 to 32, measure global pool miss rate
3. **NUMA sensitivity**: Pin threads to cores on different sockets, measure cross-socket access overhead
4. **Metrics**: Track local-cache hits, global-pool fetches, CAS retry rate, and allocation/free latency percentiles

## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of NUMA-aware memory management and lock-free structures beyond simple queues. Candidates must justify locality decisions, reclamation delay trade-offs, and fragmentation management under mixed workloads. The prompt also probes API design, minimal synchronization strategies, and practical benchmarking methodology.

## Key Concepts

- **NUMA locality**: Allocations should prefer the local node to avoid expensive remote memory access
- **Slab allocator design**: Pre-allocates memory in fixed-size slabs per object size class
- **Size bucketing**: Groups objects into size classes (32–128B, 1–4KB) to reduce fragmentation
- **Lock-free free lists**: CAS-based singly-linked lists enable concurrent access without heavy locking
- **Cross-node reclamation**: Deferred reclamation via epoch orquEUe avoids false sharing and reduces ping-ponging
- **Alignment and false sharing**: Cache-line padding prevents unrelated objects from sharing the same line
- **TLB/cache effects**: Slab locality improves cache hit rates; large pages (huge TLB entries) reduce TLB misses for bulk operations

## Code Example

```c
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

typedef enum { SMALL_32B, SMALL_64B, SMALL_128B, MEDIUM_1KB, MEDIUM_4KB, NUM_BUCKETS } bucket_t;

typedef struct free_node {
    struct free_node *next;
} free_node_t;

typedef struct {
    free_node_t *head;
    _Atomic uint64_t epoch;
    char pad[64 - sizeof(void*) - sizeof(_Atomic uint64_t)];
} alignas(64) lockfree_freelist_t;

typedef struct {
    lockfree_freelist_t freelists[NUM_BUCKETS];
    void *slab_start;
    size_t slab_size;
    int node_id;
    bool preallocate;
} alignas(64) numa_slab_cache_t;

typedef struct {
    numa_slab_cache_t *caches;
    int num_nodes;
    size_t page_size;
    void *(*numa_alloc)(size_t size, int node);
    void (*numa_free)(void *ptr, size_t size);
} numa_allocator_t;

numa_allocator_t *numa_allocator_create(int num_nodes, void *(*alloc_fn)(size_t, int), void (*free_fn)(void*, size_t));
void *numa_alloc(numa_allocator_t *alloc, size_t size, int node_hint);
void numa_free(numa_allocator_t *alloc, void *ptr, size_t size, int target_node);
void numa_allocator_destroy(numa_allocator_t *alloc);
```

## Fragmentation Considerations

- **Internal fragmentation**: Inherent in fixed-size bucketing; acceptable given latency requirements
- **External fragmentation**: Mitigated by pre-allocated slabs and lazy reclamation (freed objects held until next allocation)
- **Cross-node fragmentation**: Remote frees accumulate in target node queues; monitored via periodic compaction

## TLB/Cache Effects

- Slab allocation ensures objects within a slab share TLB entries
- Cache-line-aligned objects prevent false sharing in multiprocess workloads
- Large pages (2MB/1GB) recommended for bulk allocations to reduce TLB pressure
- NUMA interleaving disabled for hot paths; manual placement preferred

## Microbenchmark Plan

| Test | Workload | Metrics |
|------|----------|---------|
| 1. Local-only | Allocate/free on local node | Latency p50/p99/p999 |
| 2. Remote-heavy | 90% cross-node frees | Remote access ratio, latency delta |
| 3. Mixed sizes | Uniform distribution across buckets | Fragmentation index |
| 4. Contention | High thread count, shared buckets | Throughput, lock convoy detection |
| 5. Reclaim storm | Bulk free across nodes | Reclamation latency, stall time |

Measure under steady-state after warmup; use perf stat for cache-miss rates and TLB miss ratios.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical low-level synchronization and memory-order reasoning.

## Key Concepts
- Ticket-based spinlock yields FIFO fairness
- Atomics: fetch_add, load, store
- Memory orders: acquire on lock, release on unlock, relaxed during waiting

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct {
  _Atomic uint32_t next;
  _Atomic uint32_t serve;
} TicketLock;
void init(TicketLock* t){ atomic_store(&t->next,0); atomic_store(&t->serve,0); }
uint32_t lock(TicketLock* t){ uint32_t my = atomic_fetch_add(&t->next,1); while (atomic_load(&t->serve) != my) { _mm_pause(); } return my; }
void unlock(TicketLock* t, uint32_t my){ atomic_store_explicit(&t->serve, my+1, memory_order_release); }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for NUMA or many threads?
- What are practical drawbacks of busy-waiting and how would you verify FIFO fairness under stress?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses low-latency concurrency, NUMA awareness, and cache-friendly data structures.

## Key Concepts
- NUMA locality and cache-line alignment
- Lock-free open addressing with CAS
- ABA avoidance via version counters
- Safe memory reclamation (epoch-based)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode for a slot
class Slot {
  constructor() {
    this.key = 0n; // BigInt for 64-bit
    this.val = 0n;
    this.seq = 0n;
  }
}

function slot_insert(slot, k, v) {
  const cur = atomicLoad(slot.seq);
  if (cur !== 0n) return false;
  if (atomicCompareExchange(slot.seq, cur, 1n)) {
    slot.key = k;
    slot.val = v;
    atomicStore(slot.seq, cur + 1n);
    return true;
  }
  return false;
}

function slot_get(slot, k) {
  const cur = atomicLoad(slot.seq);
  if ((cur & 1n) === 0n) return { found: false };
  if (slot.key === k) return { found: true, val: slot.val };
  return { found: false };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 3 sockets and handle memory bandwidth saturation?

## Why This Is Asked
Real-world systems demand ultra-low latency allocations with NUMA awareness. A per-core cache reduces cross-socket traffic; a lock-free global pool avoids stalls under high contention; tagged pointers prevent ABA without heavy marshaling.

## Key Concepts
- Per-core caches and local allocation path
- Lock-free free lists with ABA avoidance via pointer tagging
- Cross-socket NUMA considerations and cache-line alignment
- Reclamation strategy to minimize fragmentation and stalls

## Code Example
